From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:33:57 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] [ppc64] Add a ppc64le target (generic C, no assembly)

include/openssl/target.h ends in #error "Unknown target CPU" for anything
not on its list, which since the removal of the ppc64le assembly no longer
includes ppc64le. Every consumer of BoringSSL on ppc64le therefore fails
to compile; for the Dart SDK that is dart:io's TLS (runtime/bin and the
VM itself link BoringSSL).

Recognise little-endian 64-bit POWER as OPENSSL_64_BIT + OPENSSL_PPC64LE.
No assembly is built for it (the GAS sources are all wrapped in their own
processor checks), so the generic C implementations are used. As the
comment above the #error says, patching this list is the expected local
change for an unsupported machine.

Class: local workaround for BoringSSL (upstream dropped ppc64le); Debian
chromium carries an equivalent, larger patch that also restores the
assembly.
---
 include/openssl/target.h | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/third_party/boringssl/src/include/openssl/target.h b/third_party/boringssl/src/include/openssl/target.h
index 8d4763d..3eccaf0 100644
--- a/third_party/boringssl/src/include/openssl/target.h
+++ b/third_party/boringssl/src/include/openssl/target.h
@@ -45,6 +45,10 @@
 #define OPENSSL_RISCV64
 #elif defined(__riscv) && __SIZEOF_POINTER__ == 4
 #define OPENSSL_32_BIT
+#elif defined(__powerpc64__) && defined(__LITTLE_ENDIAN__)
+// ppc64le (ELFv2). No assembly: the generic C implementations are used.
+#define OPENSSL_64_BIT
+#define OPENSSL_PPC64LE
 #elif defined(__pnacl__)
 #define OPENSSL_32_BIT
 #define OPENSSL_PNACL
